Sending: CHAPTER I.
Sending: Down the Rabbit-Hole
Sending: 
Sending: 
Sending: Alice was beginning to get very tired of sitting by her sister on the
Sending: bank, and of having nothing to do: once or twice she had peeped into
Sending: the book her sister was reading, but it had no pictures or
Sending: conversations in it, “and what is the use of a book,” thought Alice
Sending: “without pictures or conversations?”
Sending: 
Sending: So she was considering in her own mind (as well as she could, for the
Sending: hot day made her feel very sleepy and stupid), whether the pleasure of
Sending: making a daisy-chain would be worth the trouble of getting up and
Sending: picking the daisies, when suddenly a White Rabbit with pink eyes ran
Sending: close by her.
Sending: 
Sending: There was nothing so _very_ remarkable in that; nor did Alice think it
Sending: so _very_ much out of the way to hear the Rabbit say to itself, “Oh
Sending: dear! Oh dear! I shall be late!” (when she thought it over afterwards,
Sending: it occurred to her that she ought to have wondered at this, but at the
Sending: time it all seemed quite natural); but when the Rabbit actually _took a
Sending: watch out of its waistcoat-pocket_, and looked at it, and then hurried
Sending: on, Alice started to her feet, for it flashed across her mind that she
Sending: had never before seen a rabbit with either a waistcoat-pocket, or a
Sending: watch to take out of it, and burning with curiosity, she ran across the
Sending: field after it, and fortunately was just in time to see it pop down a
Sending: large rabbit-hole under the hedge.
Sending: 
Sending: In another moment down went Alice after it, never once considering how
Sending: in the world she was to get out again.
Sending: 
Sending: The rabbit-hole went straight on like a tunnel for some way, and then
Sending: dipped suddenly down, so suddenly that Alice had not a moment to think
Sending: about stopping herself before she found herself falling down a very
Sending: deep well.
Sending: 
Sending: Either the well was very deep, or she fell very slowly, for she had
Sending: plenty of time as she went down to look about her and to wonder what
Sending: was going to happen next. First, she tried to look down and make out
Sending: what she was coming to, but it was too dark to see anything; then she
Sending: looked at the sides of the well, and noticed that they were filled with
Sending: cupboards and book-shelves; here and there she saw maps and pictures
Sending: hung upon pegs. She took down a jar from one of the shelves as she
Sending: passed; it was labelled “ORANGE MARMALADE”, but to her great
Sending: disappointment it was empty: she did not like to drop the jar for fear
Sending: of killing somebody underneath, so managed to put it into one of the
Sending: cupboards as she fell past it.
Sending: 
Sending: “Well!” thought Alice to herself, “after such a fall as this, I shall
Sending: think nothing of tumbling down stairs! How brave they’ll all think me
Sending: at home! Why, I wouldn’t say anything about it, even if I fell off the
Sending: top of the house!” (Which was very likely true.)
Sending: 
Sending: Down, down, down. Would the fall _never_ come to an end? “I wonder how
Sending: many miles I’ve fallen by this time?” she said aloud. “I must be
Sending: getting somewhere near the centre of the earth. Let me see: that would
Sending: be four thousand miles down, I think—” (for, you see, Alice had learnt
Sending: several things of this sort in her lessons in the schoolroom, and
Sending: though this was not a _very_ good opportunity for showing off her
Sending: knowledge, as there was no one to listen to her, still it was good
Sending: practice to say it over) “—yes, that’s about the right distance—but
Sending: then I wonder what Latitude or Longitude I’ve got to?” (Alice had no
Sending: idea what Latitude was, or Longitude either, but thought they were nice
Sending: grand words to say.)
Sending: 
Sending: Presently she began again. “I wonder if I shall fall right _through_
Sending: the earth! How funny it’ll seem to come out among the people that walk
Sending: with their heads downward! The Antipathies, I think—” (she was rather
Sending: glad there _was_ no one listening, this time, as it didn’t sound at all
Sending: the right word) “—but I shall have to ask them what the name of the
Sending: country is, you know. Please, Ma’am, is this New Zealand or Australia?”
Sending: (and she tried to curtsey as she spoke—fancy _curtseying_ as you’re
Sending: falling through the air! Do you think you could manage it?) “And what
Sending: an ignorant little girl she’ll think me for asking! No, it’ll never do
Sending: to ask: perhaps I shall see it written up somewhere.”
Sending: 
Sending: Down, down, down. There was nothing else to do, so Alice soon began
Sending: talking again. “Dinah’ll miss me very much to-night, I should think!”
Sending: (Dinah was the cat.) “I hope they’ll remember her saucer of milk at
Sending: tea-time. Dinah my dear! I wish you were down here with me! There are
Sending: no mice in the air, I’m afraid, but you might catch a bat, and that’s
Sending: very like a mouse, you know. But do cats eat bats, I wonder?” And here
Sending: Alice began to get rather sleepy, and went on saying to herself, in a
Sending: dreamy sort of way, “Do cats eat bats? Do cats eat bats?” and
Sending: sometimes, “Do bats eat cats?” for, you see, as she couldn’t answer
Sending: either question, it didn’t much matter which way she put it. She felt
Sending: that she was dozing off, and had just begun to dream that she was
Sending: walking hand in hand with Dinah, and saying to her very earnestly,
Sending: “Now, Dinah, tell me the truth: did you ever eat a bat?” when suddenly,
Sending: thump! thump! down she came upon a heap of sticks and dry leaves, and
Sending: the fall was over.
Sending: 
Sending: Alice was not a bit hurt, and she jumped up on to her feet in a moment:
Sending: she looked up, but it was all dark overhead; before her was another
Sending: long passage, and the White Rabbit was still in sight, hurrying down
Sending: it. There was not a moment to be lost: away went Alice like the wind,
Sending: and was just in time to hear it say, as it turned a corner, “Oh my ears
Sending: and whiskers, how late it’s getting!” She was close behind it when she
Sending: turned the corner, but the Rabbit was no longer to be seen: she found
Sending: herself in a long, low hall, which was lit up by a row of lamps hanging
Sending: from the roof.
Sending: 
Sending: There were doors all round the hall, but they were all locked; and when
Sending: Alice had been all the way down one side and up the other, trying every
Sending: door, she walked sadly down the middle, wondering how she was ever to
Sending: get out again.
Sending: 
Sending: Suddenly she came upon a little three-legged table, all made of solid
Sending: glass; there was nothing on it except a tiny golden key, and Alice’s
Sending: first thought was that it might belong to one of the doors of the hall;
Sending: but, alas! either the locks were too large, or the key was too small,
Sending: but at any rate it would not open any of them. However, on the second
Sending: time round, she came upon a low curtain she had not noticed before, and
Sending: behind it was a little door about fifteen inches high: she tried the
Sending: little golden key in the lock, and to her great delight it fitted!
Sending: 
Sending: Alice opened the door and found that it led into a small passage, not
Sending: much larger than a rat-hole: she knelt down and looked along the
Sending: passage into the loveliest garden you ever saw. How she longed to get
Sending: out of that dark hall, and wander about among those beds of bright
Sending: flowers and those cool fountains, but she could not even get her head
Sending: through the doorway; “and even if my head would go through,” thought
Sending: poor Alice, “it would be of very little use without my shoulders. Oh,
Sending: how I wish I could shut up like a telescope! I think I could, if I only
Sending: knew how to begin.” For, you see, so many out-of-the-way things had
Sending: happened lately, that Alice had begun to think that very few things
Sending: indeed were really impossible.
Sending: 
Sending: There seemed to be no use in waiting by the little door, so she went
Sending: back to the table, half hoping she might find another key on it, or at
Sending: any rate a book of rules for shutting people up like telescopes: this
Sending: time she found a little bottle on it, (“which certainly was not here
Sending: before,” said Alice,) and round the neck of the bottle was a paper
Sending: label, with the words “DRINK ME,” beautifully printed on it in large
Sending: letters.
Sending: 
Sending: It was all very well to say “Drink me,” but the wise little Alice was
Sending: not going to do _that_ in a hurry. “No, I’ll look first,” she said,
Sending: “and see whether it’s marked ‘_poison_’ or not”; for she had read
Sending: several nice little histories about children who had got burnt, and
Sending: eaten up by wild beasts and other unpleasant things, all because they
Sending: _would_ not remember the simple rules their friends had taught them:
Sending: such as, that a red-hot poker will burn you if you hold it too long;
Sending: and that if you cut your finger _very_ deeply with a knife, it usually
Sending: bleeds; and she had never forgotten that, if you drink much from a
Sending: bottle marked “poison,” it is almost certain to disagree with you,
Sending: sooner or later.
Sending: 
Sending: However, this bottle was _not_ marked “poison,” so Alice ventured to
Sending: taste it, and finding it very nice, (it had, in fact, a sort of mixed
Sending: flavour of cherry-tart, custard, pine-apple, roast turkey, toffee, and
Sending: hot buttered toast,) she very soon finished it off.
Sending: 
