Sending: 
Sending: “What a curious feeling!” said Alice; “I must be shutting up like a
Sending: telescope.”
Sending: 
Sending: And so it was indeed: she was now only ten inches high, and her face
Sending: brightened up at the thought that she was now the right size for going
Sending: through the little door into that lovely garden. First, however, she
Sending: waited for a few minutes to see if she was going to shrink any further:
Sending: she felt a little nervous about this; “for it might end, you know,”
Sending: said Alice to herself, “in my going out altogether, like a candle. I
Sending: wonder what I should be like then?” And she tried to fancy what the
Sending: flame of a candle is like after the candle is blown out, for she could
Sending: not remember ever having seen such a thing.
Sending: 
Sending: After a while, finding that nothing more happened, she decided on going
Sending: into the garden at once; but, alas for poor Alice! when she got to the
Sending: door, she found she had forgotten the little golden key, and when she
Sending: went back to the table for it, she found she could not possibly reach
Sending: it: she could see it quite plainly through the glass, and she tried her
Sending: best to climb up one of the legs of the table, but it was too slippery;
Sending: and when she had tired herself out with trying, the poor little thing
Sending: sat down and cried.
Sending: 
Sending: “Come, there’s no use in crying like that!” said Alice to herself,
Sending: rather sharply; “I advise you to leave off this minute!” She generally
Sending: gave herself very good advice, (though she very seldom followed it),
Sending: and sometimes she scolded herself so severely as to bring tears into
Sending: her eyes; and once she remembered trying to box her own ears for having
Sending: cheated herself in a game of croquet she was playing against herself,
Sending: for this curious child was very fond of pretending to be two people.
Sending: “But it’s no use now,” thought poor Alice, “to pretend to be two
Sending: people! Why, there’s hardly enough of me left to make _one_ respectable
Sending: person!”
Sending: 
Sending: Soon her eye fell on a little glass box that was lying under the table:
Sending: she opened it, and found in it a very small cake, on which the words
Sending: “EAT ME” were beautifully marked in currants. “Well, I’ll eat it,” said
Sending: Alice, “and if it makes me grow larger, I can reach the key; and if it
Sending: makes me grow smaller, I can creep under the door; so either way I’ll
Sending: get into the garden, and I don’t care which happens!”
Sending: 
Sending: She ate a little bit, and said anxiously to herself, “Which way? Which
Sending: way?”, holding her hand on the top of her head to feel which way it was
Sending: growing, and she was quite surprised to find that she remained the same
Sending: size: to be sure, this generally happens when one eats cake, but Alice
Sending: had got so much into the way of expecting nothing but out-of-the-way
Sending: things to happen, that it seemed quite dull and stupid for life to go
Sending: on in the common way.
Sending: 
Sending: So she set to work, and very soon finished off the cake.
