Sending: CHAPTER II.
Sending: The Pool of Tears
Sending: 
Sending: 
Sending: “Curiouser and curiouser!” cried Alice (she was so much surprised, that
Sending: for the moment she quite forgot how to speak good English); “now I’m
Sending: opening out like the largest telescope that ever was! Good-bye, feet!”
Sending: (for when she looked down at her feet, they seemed to be almost out of
Sending: sight, they were getting so far off). “Oh, my poor little feet, I
Sending: wonder who will put on your shoes and stockings for you now, dears? I’m
Sending: sure _I_ shan’t be able! I shall be a great deal too far off to trouble
Sending: myself about you: you must manage the best way you can;—but I must be
Sending: kind to them,” thought Alice, “or perhaps they won’t walk the way I
Sending: want to go! Let me see: I’ll give them a new pair of boots every
Sending: Christmas.”
Sending: 
Sending: And she went on planning to herself how she would manage it. “They must
Sending: go by the carrier,” she thought; “and how funny it’ll seem, sending
Sending: presents to one’s own feet! And how odd the directions will look!
Sending: 
Sending:      _Alice’s Right Foot, Esq., Hearthrug, near the Fender,_ (_with
Sending:      Alice’s love_).
Sending: 
Sending: Oh dear, what nonsense I’m talking!”
Sending: 
Sending: Just then her head struck against the roof of the hall: in fact she was
Sending: now more than nine feet high, and she at once took up the little golden
Sending: key and hurried off to the garden door.
Sending: 
Sending: Poor Alice! It was as much as she could do, lying down on one side, to
Sending: look through into the garden with one eye; but to get through was more
Sending: hopeless than ever: she sat down and began to cry again.
Sending: 
Sending: “You ought to be ashamed of yourself,” said Alice, “a great girl like
Sending: you,” (she might well say this), “to go on crying in this way! Stop
Sending: this moment, I tell you!” But she went on all the same, shedding
Sending: gallons of tears, until there was a large pool all round her, about
Sending: four inches deep and reaching half down the hall.
Sending: 
Sending: After a time she heard a little pattering of feet in the distance, and
Sending: she hastily dried her eyes to see what was coming. It was the White
Sending: Rabbit returning, splendidly dressed, with a pair of white kid gloves
Sending: in one hand and a large fan in the other: he came trotting along in a
Sending: great hurry, muttering to himself as he came, “Oh! the Duchess, the
Sending: Duchess! Oh! won’t she be savage if I’ve kept her waiting!” Alice felt
Sending: so desperate that she was ready to ask help of any one; so, when the
Sending: Rabbit came near her, she began, in a low, timid voice, “If you please,
Sending: sir—” The Rabbit started violently, dropped the white kid gloves and
Sending: the fan, and skurried away into the darkness as hard as he could go.
Sending: 
Sending: Alice took up the fan and gloves, and, as the hall was very hot, she
Sending: kept fanning herself all the time she went on talking: “Dear, dear! How
Sending: queer everything is to-day! And yesterday things went on just as usual.
Sending: I wonder if I’ve been changed in the night? Let me think: was I the
Sending: same when I got up this morning? I almost think I can remember feeling
Sending: a little different. But if I’m not the same, the next question is, Who
Sending: in the world am I? Ah, _that’s_ the great puzzle!” And she began
Sending: thinking over all the children she knew that were of the same age as
Sending: herself, to see if she could have been changed for any of them.
Sending: 
Sending: “I’m sure I’m not Ada,” she said, “for her hair goes in such long
Sending: ringlets, and mine doesn’t go in ringlets at all; and I’m sure I can’t
Sending: be Mabel, for I know all sorts of things, and she, oh! she knows such a
Sending: very little! Besides, _she’s_ she, and _I’m_ I, and—oh dear, how
Sending: puzzling it all is! I’ll try if I know all the things I used to know.
Sending: Let me see: four times five is twelve, and four times six is thirteen,
Sending: and four times seven is—oh dear! I shall never get to twenty at that
Sending: rate! However, the Multiplication Table doesn’t signify: let’s try
Sending: Geography. London is the capital of Paris, and Paris is the capital of
Sending: Rome, and Rome—no, _that’s_ all wrong, I’m certain! I must have been
Sending: changed for Mabel! I’ll try and say ‘_How doth the little_—’” and she
Sending: crossed her hands on her lap as if she were saying lessons, and began
Sending: to repeat it, but her voice sounded hoarse and strange, and the words
Sending: did not come the same as they used to do:—
Sending: 
Sending: “How doth the little crocodile
Sending:     Improve his shining tail,
Sending: And pour the waters of the Nile
Sending:     On every golden scale!
Sending: 
Sending: “How cheerfully he seems to grin,
Sending:     How neatly spread his claws,
Sending: And welcome little fishes in
Sending:     With gently smiling jaws!”
Sending: 
Sending: 
Sending: “I’m sure those are not the right words,” said poor Alice, and her eyes
Sending: filled with tears again as she went on, “I must be Mabel after all, and
Sending: I shall have to go and live in that poky little house, and have next to
Sending: no toys to play with, and oh! ever so many lessons to learn! No, I’ve
Sending: made up my mind about it; if I’m Mabel, I’ll stay down here! It’ll be
Sending: no use their putting their heads down and saying ‘Come up again, dear!’
Sending: I shall only look up and say ‘Who am I then? Tell me that first, and
Sending: then, if I like being that person, I’ll come up: if not, I’ll stay down
Sending: here till I’m somebody else’—but, oh dear!” cried Alice, with a sudden
Sending: burst of tears, “I do wish they _would_ put their heads down! I am so
Sending: _very_ tired of being all alone here!”
Sending: 
Sending: As she said this she looked down at her hands, and was surprised to see
Sending: that she had put on one of the Rabbit’s little white kid gloves while
Sending: she was talking. “How _can_ I have done that?” she thought. “I must be
Sending: growing small again.” She got up and went to the table to measure
Sending: herself by it, and found that, as nearly as she could guess, she was
Sending: now about two feet high, and was going on shrinking rapidly: she soon
Sending: found out that the cause of this was the fan she was holding, and she
Sending: dropped it hastily, just in time to avoid shrinking away altogether.
Sending: 
Sending: “That _was_ a narrow escape!” said Alice, a good deal frightened at the
Sending: sudden change, but very glad to find herself still in existence; “and
Sending: now for the garden!” and she ran with all speed back to the little
Sending: door: but, alas! the little door was shut again, and the little golden
Sending: key was lying on the glass table as before, “and things are worse than
Sending: ever,” thought the poor child, “for I never was so small as this
Sending: before, never! And I declare it’s too bad, that it is!”
Sending: 
Sending: As she said these words her foot slipped, and in another moment,
Sending: splash! she was up to her chin in salt water. Her first idea was that
Sending: she had somehow fallen into the sea, “and in that case I can go back by
Sending: railway,” she said to herself. (Alice had been to the seaside once in
Sending: her life, and had come to the general conclusion, that wherever you go
Sending: to on the English coast you find a number of bathing machines in the
Sending: sea, some children digging in the sand with wooden spades, then a row
Sending: of lodging houses, and behind them a railway station.) However, she
Sending: soon made out that she was in the pool of tears which she had wept when
Sending: she was nine feet high.
Sending: 
Sending: “I wish I hadn’t cried so much!” said Alice, as she swam about, trying
Sending: to find her way out. “I shall be punished for it now, I suppose, by
Sending: being drowned in my own tears! That _will_ be a queer thing, to be
Sending: sure! However, everything is queer to-day.”
Sending: 
Sending: Just then she heard something splashing about in the pool a little way
Sending: off, and she swam nearer to make out what it was: at first she thought
Sending: it must be a walrus or hippopotamus, but then she remembered how small
Sending: she was now, and she soon made out that it was only a mouse that had
Sending: slipped in like herself.
Sending: 
Sending: “Would it be of any use, now,” thought Alice, “to speak to this mouse?
Sending: Everything is so out-of-the-way down here, that I should think very
Sending: likely it can talk: at any rate, there’s no harm in trying.” So she
Sending: began: “O Mouse, do you know the way out of this pool? I am very tired
Sending: of swimming about here, O Mouse!” (Alice thought this must be the right
Sending: way of speaking to a mouse: she had never done such a thing before, but
Sending: she remembered having seen in her brother’s Latin Grammar, “A mouse—of
Sending: a mouse—to a mouse—a mouse—O mouse!”) The Mouse looked at her rather
Sending: inquisitively, and seemed to her to wink with one of its little eyes,
Sending: but it said nothing.
Sending: 
Sending: “Perhaps it doesn’t understand English,” thought Alice; “I daresay it’s
Sending: a French mouse, come over with William the Conqueror.” (For, with all
Sending: her knowledge of history, Alice had no very clear notion how long ago
