Sending: was the first sentence in her French lesson-book. The Mouse gave a
Sending: sudden leap out of the water, and seemed to quiver all over with
Sending: fright. “Oh, I beg your pardon!” cried Alice hastily, afraid that she
Sending: had hurt the poor animal’s feelings. “I quite forgot you didn’t like
Sending: cats.”
Sending: 
Sending: “Not like cats!” cried the Mouse, in a shrill, passionate voice. “Would
Sending: _you_ like cats if you were me?”
Sending: 
Sending: “Well, perhaps not,” said Alice in a soothing tone: “don’t be angry
Sending: about it. And yet I wish I could show you our cat Dinah: I think you’d
Sending: take a fancy to cats if you could only see her. She is such a dear
Sending: quiet thing,” Alice went on, half to herself, as she swam lazily about
Sending: in the pool, “and she sits purring so nicely by the fire, licking her
Sending: paws and washing her face—and she is such a nice soft thing to
