Sending: CHAPTER III.
Sending: A Caucus-Race and a Long Tale
Sending: 
Sending: 
Sending: They were indeed a queer-looking party that assembled on the bank—the
Sending: birds with draggled feathers, the animals with their fur clinging close
Sending: to them, and all dripping wet, cross, and uncomfortable.
Sending: 
Sending: The first question of course was, how to get dry again: they had a
Sending: consultation about this, and after a few minutes it seemed quite
Sending: natural to Alice to find herself talking familiarly with them, as if
Sending: she had known them all her life. Indeed, she had quite a long argument
Sending: with the Lory, who at last turned sulky, and would only say, “I am
Sending: older than you, and must know better;” and this Alice would not allow
Sending: without knowing how old it was, and, as the Lory positively refused to
Sending: tell its age, there was no more to be said.
Sending: 
Sending: At last the Mouse, who seemed to be a person of authority among them,
Sending: called out, “Sit down, all of you, and listen to me! _I’ll_ soon make
Sending: you dry enough!” They all sat down at once, in a large ring, with the
Sending: Mouse in the middle. Alice kept her eyes anxiously fixed on it, for she
Sending: felt sure she would catch a bad cold if she did not get dry very soon.
Sending: 
Sending: “Ahem!” said the Mouse with an important air, “are you all ready? This
Sending: is the driest thing I know. Silence all round, if you please! ‘William
Sending: the Conqueror, whose cause was favoured by the pope, was soon submitted
Sending: to by the English, who wanted leaders, and had been of late much
Sending: accustomed to usurpation and conquest. Edwin and Morcar, the earls of
Sending: Mercia and Northumbria—’”
Sending: 
Sending: “Ugh!” said the Lory, with a shiver.
Sending: 
Sending: “I beg your pardon!” said the Mouse, frowning, but very politely: “Did
Sending: you speak?”
Sending: 
Sending: “Not I!” said the Lory hastily.
Sending: 
Sending: “I thought you did,” said the Mouse. “—I proceed. ‘Edwin and Morcar,
Sending: the earls of Mercia and Northumbria, declared for him: and even
Sending: Stigand, the patriotic archbishop of Canterbury, found it advisable—’”
