Sending: CHAPTER IV.
Sending: The Rabbit Sends in a Little Bill
Sending: 
Sending: 
Sending: It was the White Rabbit, trotting slowly back again, and looking
Sending: anxiously about as it went, as if it had lost something; and she heard
Sending: it muttering to itself “The Duchess! The Duchess! Oh my dear paws! Oh
Sending: my fur and whiskers! She’ll get me executed, as sure as ferrets are
Sending: ferrets! Where _can_ I have dropped them, I wonder?” Alice guessed in a
Sending: moment that it was looking for the fan and the pair of white kid
Sending: gloves, and she very good-naturedly began hunting about for them, but
Sending: they were nowhere to be seen—everything seemed to have changed since
Sending: her swim in the pool, and the great hall, with the glass table and the
Sending: little door, had vanished completely.
Sending: 
Sending: Very soon the Rabbit noticed Alice, as she went hunting about, and
Sending: called out to her in an angry tone, “Why, Mary Ann, what _are_ you
Sending: doing out here? Run home this moment, and fetch me a pair of gloves and
Sending: a fan! Quick, now!” And Alice was so much frightened that she ran off
Sending: at once in the direction it pointed to, without trying to explain the
Sending: mistake it had made.
Sending: 
Sending: “He took me for his housemaid,” she said to herself as she ran. “How
Sending: surprised he’ll be when he finds out who I am! But I’d better take him
Sending: his fan and gloves—that is, if I can find them.” As she said this, she
Sending: came upon a neat little house, on the door of which was a bright brass
Sending: plate with the name “W. RABBIT,” engraved upon it. She went in without
Sending: knocking, and hurried upstairs, in great fear lest she should meet the
Sending: real Mary Ann, and be turned out of the house before she had found the
Sending: fan and gloves.
Sending: 
Sending: “How queer it seems,” Alice said to herself, “to be going messages for
Sending: a rabbit! I suppose Dinah’ll be sending me on messages next!” And she
Sending: began fancying the sort of thing that would happen: “‘Miss Alice! Come
Sending: here directly, and get ready for your walk!’ ‘Coming in a minute,
Sending: nurse! But I’ve got to see that the mouse doesn’t get out.’ Only I
Sending: don’t think,” Alice went on, “that they’d let Dinah stop in the house
Sending: if it began ordering people about like that!”
Sending: 
Sending: By this time she had found her way into a tidy little room with a table
Sending: in the window, and on it (as she had hoped) a fan and two or three
Sending: pairs of tiny white kid gloves: she took up the fan and a pair of the
Sending: gloves, and was just going to leave the room, when her eye fell upon a
Sending: little bottle that stood near the looking-glass. There was no label
Sending: this time with the words “DRINK ME,” but nevertheless she uncorked it
Sending: and put it to her lips. “I know _something_ interesting is sure to
Sending: happen,” she said to herself, “whenever I eat or drink anything; so
Sending: I’ll just see what this bottle does. I do hope it’ll make me grow large
Sending: again, for really I’m quite tired of being such a tiny little thing!”
Sending: 
Sending: It did so indeed, and much sooner than she had expected: before she had
Sending: drunk half the bottle, she found her head pressing against the ceiling,
Sending: and had to stoop to save her neck from being broken. She hastily put
Sending: down the bottle, saying to herself “That’s quite enough—I hope I shan’t
Sending: grow any more—As it is, I can’t get out at the door—I do wish I hadn’t
Sending: drunk quite so much!”
Sending: 
Sending: Alas! it was too late to wish that! She went on growing, and growing,
Sending: and very soon had to kneel down on the floor: in another minute there
Sending: was not even room for this, and she tried the effect of lying down with
Sending: one elbow against the door, and the other arm curled round her head.
Sending: Still she went on growing, and, as a last resource, she put one arm out
Sending: of the window, and one foot up the chimney, and said to herself “Now I
Sending: can do no more, whatever happens. What _will_ become of me?”
Sending: 
Sending: Luckily for Alice, the little magic bottle had now had its full effect,
Sending: and she grew no larger: still it was very uncomfortable, and, as there
Sending: seemed to be no sort of chance of her ever getting out of the room
Sending: again, no wonder she felt unhappy.
Sending: 
Sending: “It was much pleasanter at home,” thought poor Alice, “when one wasn’t
Sending: always growing larger and smaller, and being ordered about by mice and
Sending: rabbits. I almost wish I hadn’t gone down that rabbit-hole—and yet—and
