Sending: suppose I ought to eat or drink something or other; but the great
Sending: question is, what?”
Sending: 
Sending: The great question certainly was, what? Alice looked all round her at
Sending: the flowers and the blades of grass, but she did not see anything that
Sending: looked like the right thing to eat or drink under the circumstances.
Sending: There was a large mushroom growing near her, about the same height as
Sending: herself; and when she had looked under it, and on both sides of it, and
Sending: behind it, it occurred to her that she might as well look and see what
Sending: was on the top of it.
Sending: 
Sending: She stretched herself up on tiptoe, and peeped over the edge of the
Sending: mushroom, and her eyes immediately met those of a large blue
Sending: caterpillar, that was sitting on the top with its arms folded, quietly
Sending: smoking a long hookah, and taking not the smallest notice of her or of
Sending: anything else.
Sending: 
Sending: 
Sending: 
Sending: 
Sending: CHAPTER V.
Sending: Advice from a Caterpillar
Sending: 
Sending: 
Sending: The Caterpillar and Alice looked at each other for some time in
Sending: silence: at last the Caterpillar took the hookah out of its mouth, and
Sending: addressed her in a languid, sleepy voice.
Sending: 
Sending: “Who are _you?_” said the Caterpillar.
Sending: 
Sending: This was not an encouraging opening for a conversation. Alice replied,
Sending: rather shyly, “I—I hardly know, sir, just at present—at least I know
Sending: who I _was_ when I got up this morning, but I think I must have been
Sending: changed several times since then.”
Sending: 
Sending: “What do you mean by that?” said the Caterpillar sternly. “Explain
Sending: yourself!”
Sending: 
Sending: “I can’t explain _myself_, I’m afraid, sir,” said Alice, “because I’m
Sending: not myself, you see.”
Sending: 
Sending: “I don’t see,” said the Caterpillar.
Sending: 
Sending: “I’m afraid I can’t put it more clearly,” Alice replied very politely,
Sending: “for I can’t understand it myself to begin with; and being so many
Sending: different sizes in a day is very confusing.”
Sending: 
Sending: “It isn’t,” said the Caterpillar.
Sending: 
Sending: “Well, perhaps you haven’t found it so yet,” said Alice; “but when you
Sending: have to turn into a chrysalis—you will some day, you know—and then
Sending: after that into a butterfly, I should think you’ll feel it a little
Sending: queer, won’t you?”
Sending: 
Sending: “Not a bit,” said the Caterpillar.
Sending: 
Sending: “Well, perhaps your feelings may be different,” said Alice; “all I know
Sending: is, it would feel very queer to _me_.”
Sending: 
Sending: “You!” said the Caterpillar contemptuously. “Who are _you?_”
Sending: 
Sending: Which brought them back again to the beginning of the conversation.
Sending: Alice felt a little irritated at the Caterpillar’s making such _very_
Sending: short remarks, and she drew herself up and said, very gravely, “I
Sending: think, you ought to tell me who _you_ are, first.”
Sending: 
Sending: “Why?” said the Caterpillar.
Sending: 
Sending: Here was another puzzling question; and as Alice could not think of any
Sending: good reason, and as the Caterpillar seemed to be in a _very_ unpleasant
Sending: state of mind, she turned away.
Sending: 
Sending: “Come back!” the Caterpillar called after her. “I’ve something
Sending: important to say!”
Sending: 
Sending: This sounded promising, certainly: Alice turned and came back again.
Sending: 
Sending: “Keep your temper,” said the Caterpillar.
Sending: 
Sending: “Is that all?” said Alice, swallowing down her anger as well as she
Sending: could.
Sending: 
Sending: “No,” said the Caterpillar.
Sending: 
Sending: Alice thought she might as well wait, as she had nothing else to do,
Sending: and perhaps after all it might tell her something worth hearing. For
Sending: some minutes it puffed away without speaking, but at last it unfolded
Sending: its arms, took the hookah out of its mouth again, and said, “So you
Sending: think you’re changed, do you?”
Sending: 
Sending: “I’m afraid I am, sir,” said Alice; “I can’t remember things as I
Sending: used—and I don’t keep the same size for ten minutes together!”
Sending: 
Sending: “Can’t remember _what_ things?” said the Caterpillar.
Sending: 
Sending: “Well, I’ve tried to say “How doth the little busy bee,” but it all
Sending: came different!” Alice replied in a very melancholy voice.
Sending: 
Sending: “Repeat, ‘_You are old, Father William_,’” said the Caterpillar.
Sending: 
Sending: Alice folded her hands, and began:—
Sending: 
Sending: “You are old, Father William,” the young man said,
Sending:     “And your hair has become very white;
Sending: And yet you incessantly stand on your head—
Sending:     Do you think, at your age, it is right?”
Sending: 
Sending: “In my youth,” Father William replied to his son,
Sending:     “I feared it might injure the brain;
Sending: But, now that I’m perfectly sure I have none,
Sending:     Why, I do it again and again.”
Sending: 
Sending: “You are old,” said the youth, “as I mentioned before,
Sending:     And have grown most uncommonly fat;
Sending: Yet you turned a back-somersault in at the door—
Sending:     Pray, what is the reason of that?”
Sending: 
Sending: “In my youth,” said the sage, as he shook his grey locks,
Sending:     “I kept all my limbs very supple
Sending: By the use of this ointment—one shilling the box—
Sending:     Allow me to sell you a couple?”
Sending: 
Sending: “You are old,” said the youth, “and your jaws are too weak
Sending:     For anything tougher than suet;
Sending: Yet you finished the goose, with the bones and the beak—
Sending:     Pray, how did you manage to do it?”
Sending: 
Sending: “In my youth,” said his father, “I took to the law,
Sending:     And argued each case with my wife;
