Sending: *      *      *      *      *      *      *
Sending: 
Sending: 
Sending: “Come, my head’s free at last!” said Alice in a tone of delight, which
Sending: changed into alarm in another moment, when she found that her shoulders
Sending: were nowhere to be found: all she could see, when she looked down, was
Sending: an immense length of neck, which seemed to rise like a stalk out of a
Sending: sea of green leaves that lay far below her.
Sending: 
Sending: “What _can_ all that green stuff be?” said Alice. “And where _have_ my
Sending: shoulders got to? And oh, my poor hands, how is it I can’t see you?”
Sending: She was moving them about as she spoke, but no result seemed to follow,
Sending: except a little shaking among the distant green leaves.
Sending: 
Sending: As there seemed to be no chance of getting her hands up to her head,
Sending: she tried to get her head down to them, and was delighted to find that
Sending: her neck would bend about easily in any direction, like a serpent. She
Sending: had just succeeded in curving it down into a graceful zigzag, and was
Sending: going to dive in among the leaves, which she found to be nothing but
Sending: the tops of the trees under which she had been wandering, when a sharp
Sending: hiss made her draw back in a hurry: a large pigeon had flown into her
Sending: face, and was beating her violently with its wings.
Sending: 
