Sending: CHAPTER VI.
Sending: Pig and Pepper
Sending: 
Sending: 
Sending: For a minute or two she stood looking at the house, and wondering what
Sending: to do next, when suddenly a footman in livery came running out of the
Sending: wood—(she considered him to be a footman because he was in livery:
Sending: otherwise, judging by his face only, she would have called him a
Sending: fish)—and rapped loudly at the door with his knuckles. It was opened by
Sending: another footman in livery, with a round face, and large eyes like a
Sending: frog; and both footmen, Alice noticed, had powdered hair that curled
Sending: all over their heads. She felt very curious to know what it was all
Sending: about, and crept a little way out of the wood to listen.
Sending: 
Sending: The Fish-Footman began by producing from under his arm a great letter,
Sending: nearly as large as himself, and this he handed over to the other,
Sending: saying, in a solemn tone, “For the Duchess. An invitation from the
Sending: Queen to play croquet.” The Frog-Footman repeated, in the same solemn
Sending: tone, only changing the order of the words a little, “From the Queen.
Sending: An invitation for the Duchess to play croquet.”
Sending: 
Sending: Then they both bowed low, and their curls got entangled together.
Sending: 
Sending: Alice laughed so much at this, that she had to run back into the wood
Sending: for fear of their hearing her; and when she next peeped out the
Sending: Fish-Footman was gone, and the other was sitting on the ground near the
Sending: door, staring stupidly up into the sky.
Sending: 
Sending: Alice went timidly up to the door, and knocked.
Sending: 
Sending: “There’s no sort of use in knocking,” said the Footman, “and that for
Sending: two reasons. First, because I’m on the same side of the door as you
Sending: are; secondly, because they’re making such a noise inside, no one could
Sending: possibly hear you.” And certainly there _was_ a most extraordinary
Sending: noise going on within—a constant howling and sneezing, and every now
Sending: and then a great crash, as if a dish or kettle had been broken to
Sending: pieces.
Sending: 
Sending: “Please, then,” said Alice, “how am I to get in?”
Sending: 
Sending: “There might be some sense in your knocking,” the Footman went on
Sending: without attending to her, “if we had the door between us. For instance,
Sending: if you were _inside_, you might knock, and I could let you out, you
Sending: know.” He was looking up into the sky all the time he was speaking, and
Sending: this Alice thought decidedly uncivil. “But perhaps he can’t help it,”
Sending: she said to herself; “his eyes are so _very_ nearly at the top of his
Sending: head. But at any rate he might answer questions.—How am I to get in?”
Sending: she repeated, aloud.
Sending: 
Sending: “I shall sit here,” the Footman remarked, “till tomorrow—”
Sending: 
Sending: At this moment the door of the house opened, and a large plate came
Sending: skimming out, straight at the Footman’s head: it just grazed his nose,
Sending: and broke to pieces against one of the trees behind him.
Sending: 
Sending: “—or next day, maybe,” the Footman continued in the same tone, exactly
Sending: as if nothing had happened.
Sending: 
Sending: “How am I to get in?” asked Alice again, in a louder tone.
Sending: 
Sending: “_Are_ you to get in at all?” said the Footman. “That’s the first
Sending: question, you know.”
Sending: 
Sending: It was, no doubt: only Alice did not like to be told so. “It’s really
Sending: dreadful,” she muttered to herself, “the way all the creatures argue.
Sending: It’s enough to drive one crazy!”
Sending: 
Sending: The Footman seemed to think this a good opportunity for repeating his
Sending: remark, with variations. “I shall sit here,” he said, “on and off, for
Sending: days and days.”
Sending: 
Sending: “But what am _I_ to do?” said Alice.
Sending: 
Sending: “Anything you like,” said the Footman, and began whistling.
Sending: 
Sending: “Oh, there’s no use in talking to him,” said Alice desperately: “he’s
Sending: perfectly idiotic!” And she opened the door and went in.
Sending: 
Sending: The door led right into a large kitchen, which was full of smoke from
Sending: one end to the other: the Duchess was sitting on a three-legged stool
Sending: in the middle, nursing a baby; the cook was leaning over the fire,
Sending: stirring a large cauldron which seemed to be full of soup.
Sending: 
Sending: “There’s certainly too much pepper in that soup!” Alice said to
Sending: herself, as well as she could for sneezing.
Sending: 
Sending: There was certainly too much of it in the air. Even the Duchess sneezed
Sending: occasionally; and as for the baby, it was sneezing and howling
Sending: alternately without a moment’s pause. The only things in the kitchen
Sending: that did not sneeze, were the cook, and a large cat which was sitting
Sending: on the hearth and grinning from ear to ear.
Sending: 
Sending: “Please would you tell me,” said Alice, a little timidly, for she was
Sending: not quite sure whether it was good manners for her to speak first, “why
