Sending: to say which), and they went on for some while in silence.
Sending: 
Sending: Alice was just beginning to think to herself, “Now, what am I to do
Sending: with this creature when I get it home?” when it grunted again, so
Sending: violently, that she looked down into its face in some alarm. This time
Sending: there could be _no_ mistake about it: it was neither more nor less than
Sending: a pig, and she felt that it would be quite absurd for her to carry it
Sending: further.
Sending: 
Sending: So she set the little creature down, and felt quite relieved to see it
Sending: trot away quietly into the wood. “If it had grown up,” she said to
Sending: herself, “it would have made a dreadfully ugly child: but it makes
Sending: rather a handsome pig, I think.” And she began thinking over other
Sending: children she knew, who might do very well as pigs, and was just saying
Sending: to herself, “if one only knew the right way to change them—” when she
Sending: was a little startled by seeing the Cheshire Cat sitting on a bough of
Sending: a tree a few yards off.
Sending: 
Sending: The Cat only grinned when it saw Alice. It looked good-natured, she
Sending: thought: still it had _very_ long claws and a great many teeth, so she
Sending: felt that it ought to be treated with respect.
Sending: 
Sending: “Cheshire Puss,” she began, rather timidly, as she did not at all know
Sending: whether it would like the name: however, it only grinned a little
Sending: wider. “Come, it’s pleased so far,” thought Alice, and she went on.
Sending: “Would you tell me, please, which way I ought to go from here?”
Sending: 
Sending: “That depends a good deal on where you want to get to,” said the Cat.
Sending: 
Sending: “I don’t much care where—” said Alice.
Sending: 
Sending: “Then it doesn’t matter which way you go,” said the Cat.
Sending: 
Sending: “—so long as I get _somewhere_,” Alice added as an explanation.
Sending: 
Sending: “Oh, you’re sure to do that,” said the Cat, “if you only walk long
Sending: enough.”
Sending: 
Sending: Alice felt that this could not be denied, so she tried another
Sending: question. “What sort of people live about here?”
Sending: 
Sending: “In _that_ direction,” the Cat said, waving its right paw round, “lives
Sending: a Hatter: and in _that_ direction,” waving the other paw, “lives a
Sending: March Hare. Visit either you like: they’re both mad.”
Sending: 
Sending: “But I don’t want to go among mad people,” Alice remarked.
Sending: 
Sending: “Oh, you can’t help that,” said the Cat: “we’re all mad here. I’m mad.
Sending: You’re mad.”
Sending: 
Sending: “How do you know I’m mad?” said Alice.
Sending: 
Sending: “You must be,” said the Cat, “or you wouldn’t have come here.”
Sending: 
Sending: Alice didn’t think that proved it at all; however, she went on “And how
Sending: do you know that you’re mad?”
Sending: 
Sending: “To begin with,” said the Cat, “a dog’s not mad. You grant that?”
Sending: 
Sending: “I suppose so,” said Alice.
Sending: 
Sending: “Well, then,” the Cat went on, “you see, a dog growls when it’s angry,
Sending: and wags its tail when it’s pleased. Now _I_ growl when I’m pleased,
Sending: and wag my tail when I’m angry. Therefore I’m mad.”
Sending: 
Sending: “_I_ call it purring, not growling,” said Alice.
Sending: 
Sending: “Call it what you like,” said the Cat. “Do you play croquet with the
Sending: Queen to-day?”
Sending: 
Sending: “I should like it very much,” said Alice, “but I haven’t been invited
Sending: yet.”
Sending: 
Sending: “You’ll see me there,” said the Cat, and vanished.
Sending: 
Sending: Alice was not much surprised at this, she was getting so used to queer
Sending: things happening. While she was looking at the place where it had been,
Sending: it suddenly appeared again.
Sending: 
Sending: “By-the-bye, what became of the baby?” said the Cat. “I’d nearly
Sending: forgotten to ask.”
Sending: 
Sending: “It turned into a pig,” Alice quietly said, just as if it had come back
Sending: in a natural way.
Sending: 
Sending: “I thought it would,” said the Cat, and vanished again.
Sending: 
Sending: Alice waited a little, half expecting to see it again, but it did not
Sending: appear, and after a minute or two she walked on in the direction in
Sending: which the March Hare was said to live. “I’ve seen hatters before,” she
Sending: said to herself; “the March Hare will be much the most interesting, and
Sending: perhaps as this is May it won’t be raving mad—at least not so mad as it
Sending: was in March.” As she said this, she looked up, and there was the Cat
Sending: again, sitting on a branch of a tree.
Sending: 
Sending: “Did you say pig, or fig?” said the Cat.
Sending: 
Sending: “I said pig,” replied Alice; “and I wish you wouldn’t keep appearing
Sending: and vanishing so suddenly: you make one quite giddy.”
Sending: 
Sending: “All right,” said the Cat; and this time it vanished quite slowly,
Sending: beginning with the end of the tail, and ending with the grin, which
Sending: remained some time after the rest of it had gone.
Sending: 
Sending: “Well! I’ve often seen a cat without a grin,” thought Alice; “but a
Sending: grin without a cat! It’s the most curious thing I ever saw in my life!”
Sending: 
Sending: She had not gone much farther before she came in sight of the house of
Sending: the March Hare: she thought it must be the right house, because the
Sending: chimneys were shaped like ears and the roof was thatched with fur. It
Sending: was so large a house, that she did not like to go nearer till she had
Sending: nibbled some more of the lefthand bit of mushroom, and raised herself
Sending: to about two feet high: even then she walked up towards it rather
Sending: timidly, saying to herself “Suppose it should be raving mad after all!
Sending: I almost wish I’d gone to see the Hatter instead!”
