Sending: CHAPTER VII.
Sending: A Mad Tea-Party
Sending: 
Sending: 
Sending: There was a table set out under a tree in front of the house, and the
Sending: March Hare and the Hatter were having tea at it: a Dormouse was sitting
Sending: between them, fast asleep, and the other two were using it as a
Sending: cushion, resting their elbows on it, and talking over its head. “Very
Sending: uncomfortable for the Dormouse,” thought Alice; “only, as it’s asleep,
Sending: I suppose it doesn’t mind.”
Sending: 
Sending: The table was a large one, but the three were all crowded together at
Sending: one corner of it: “No room! No room!” they cried out when they saw
Sending: Alice coming. “There’s _plenty_ of room!” said Alice indignantly, and
Sending: she sat down in a large arm-chair at one end of the table.
Sending: 
Sending: “Have some wine,” the March Hare said in an encouraging tone.
Sending: 
Sending: Alice looked all round the table, but there was nothing on it but tea.
Sending: “I don’t see any wine,” she remarked.
Sending: 
Sending: “There isn’t any,” said the March Hare.
Sending: 
Sending: “Then it wasn’t very civil of you to offer it,” said Alice angrily.
Sending: 
Sending: “It wasn’t very civil of you to sit down without being invited,” said
Sending: the March Hare.
Sending: 
Sending: “I didn’t know it was _your_ table,” said Alice; “it’s laid for a great
Sending: many more than three.”
Sending: 
Sending: “Your hair wants cutting,” said the Hatter. He had been looking at
Sending: Alice for some time with great curiosity, and this was his first
Sending: speech.
Sending: 
Sending: “You should learn not to make personal remarks,” Alice said with some
Sending: severity; “it’s very rude.”
Sending: 
Sending: The Hatter opened his eyes very wide on hearing this; but all he _said_
Sending: was, “Why is a raven like a writing-desk?”
Sending: 
Sending: “Come, we shall have some fun now!” thought Alice. “I’m glad they’ve
Sending: begun asking riddles.—I believe I can guess that,” she added aloud.
Sending: 
Sending: “Do you mean that you think you can find out the answer to it?” said
Sending: the March Hare.
Sending: 
Sending: “Exactly so,” said Alice.
Sending: 
Sending: “Then you should say what you mean,” the March Hare went on.
Sending: 
Sending: “I do,” Alice hastily replied; “at least—at least I mean what I
Sending: say—that’s the same thing, you know.”
Sending: 
Sending: “Not the same thing a bit!” said the Hatter. “You might just as well
Sending: say that ‘I see what I eat’ is the same thing as ‘I eat what I see’!”
Sending: 
Sending: “You might just as well say,” added the March Hare, “that ‘I like what
Sending: I get’ is the same thing as ‘I get what I like’!”
Sending: 
Sending: “You might just as well say,” added the Dormouse, who seemed to be
Sending: talking in his sleep, “that ‘I breathe when I sleep’ is the same thing
Sending: as ‘I sleep when I breathe’!”
Sending: 
Sending: “It _is_ the same thing with you,” said the Hatter, and here the
Sending: conversation dropped, and the party sat silent for a minute, while
Sending: Alice thought over all she could remember about ravens and
Sending: writing-desks, which wasn’t much.
Sending: 
Sending: The Hatter was the first to break the silence. “What day of the month
Sending: is it?” he said, turning to Alice: he had taken his watch out of his
Sending: pocket, and was looking at it uneasily, shaking it every now and then,
Sending: and holding it to his ear.
Sending: 
Sending: Alice considered a little, and then said “The fourth.”
Sending: 
Sending: “Two days wrong!” sighed the Hatter. “I told you butter wouldn’t suit
Sending: the works!” he added looking angrily at the March Hare.
Sending: 
Sending: “It was the _best_ butter,” the March Hare meekly replied.
Sending: 
Sending: “Yes, but some crumbs must have got in as well,” the Hatter grumbled:
Sending: “you shouldn’t have put it in with the bread-knife.”
Sending: 
Sending: The March Hare took the watch and looked at it gloomily: then he dipped
Sending: it into his cup of tea, and looked at it again: but he could think of
Sending: nothing better to say than his first remark, “It was the _best_ butter,
Sending: you know.”
Sending: 
Sending: Alice had been looking over his shoulder with some curiosity. “What a
Sending: funny watch!” she remarked. “It tells the day of the month, and doesn’t
Sending: tell what o’clock it is!”
Sending: 
Sending: “Why should it?” muttered the Hatter. “Does _your_ watch tell you what
Sending: year it is?”
Sending: 
Sending: “Of course not,” Alice replied very readily: “but that’s because it
Sending: stays the same year for such a long time together.”
Sending: 
Sending: “Which is just the case with _mine_,” said the Hatter.
Sending: 
Sending: Alice felt dreadfully puzzled, The Hatter’s remark seemed to have no
Sending: sort of meaning in it, and yet it was certainly English. “I don’t quite
Sending: understand you,” she said, as politely as she could.
Sending: 
Sending: “The Dormouse is asleep again,” said the Hatter, and he poured a little
Sending: hot tea upon its nose.
Sending: 
Sending: The Dormouse shook its head impatiently, and said, without opening its
Sending: eyes, “Of course, of course; just what I was going to remark myself.”
Sending: 
Sending: “Have you guessed the riddle yet?” the Hatter said, turning to Alice
Sending: again.
Sending: 
Sending: “No, I give it up,” Alice replied: “what’s the answer?”
Sending: 
Sending: “I haven’t the slightest idea,” said the Hatter.
Sending: 
Sending: “Nor I,” said the March Hare.
Sending: 
Sending: Alice sighed wearily. “I think you might do something better with the
Sending: time,” she said, “than waste it in asking riddles that have no
Sending: answers.”
Sending: 
Sending: “If you knew Time as well as I do,” said the Hatter, “you wouldn’t talk
Sending: about wasting _it_. It’s _him_.”
Sending: 
Sending: “I don’t know what you mean,” said Alice.
Sending: 
Sending: “Of course you don’t!” the Hatter said, tossing his head
Sending: contemptuously. “I dare say you never even spoke to Time!”
Sending: 
Sending: “Perhaps not,” Alice cautiously replied: “but I know I have to beat
Sending: time when I learn music.”
Sending: 
Sending: “Ah! that accounts for it,” said the Hatter. “He won’t stand beating.
Sending: Now, if you only kept on good terms with him, he’d do almost anything
Sending: you liked with the clock. For instance, suppose it were nine o’clock in
Sending: the morning, just time to begin lessons: you’d only have to whisper a
Sending: hint to Time, and round goes the clock in a twinkling! Half-past one,
Sending: time for dinner!”
Sending: 
Sending: (“I only wish it was,” the March Hare said to itself in a whisper.)
Sending: 
Sending: “That would be grand, certainly,” said Alice thoughtfully: “but then—I
Sending: shouldn’t be hungry for it, you know.”
Sending: 
Sending: “Not at first, perhaps,” said the Hatter: “but you could keep it to
Sending: half-past one as long as you liked.”
Sending: 
Sending: “Is that the way _you_ manage?” Alice asked.
Sending: 
Sending: The Hatter shook his head mournfully. “Not I!” he replied. “We
Sending: quarrelled last March—just before _he_ went mad, you know—” (pointing
Sending: with his tea spoon at the March Hare,) “—it was at the great concert
Sending: given by the Queen of Hearts, and I had to sing
Sending: 
Sending: ‘Twinkle, twinkle, little bat!
Sending: How I wonder what you’re at!’
Sending: 
Sending: 
Sending: You know the song, perhaps?”
Sending: 
Sending: “I’ve heard something like it,” said Alice.
Sending: 
Sending: “It goes on, you know,” the Hatter continued, “in this way:—
Sending: 
Sending: ‘Up above the world you fly,
Sending: Like a tea-tray in the sky.
Sending:                     Twinkle, twinkle—’”
Sending: 
Sending: 
Sending: Here the Dormouse shook itself, and began singing in its sleep
Sending: “_Twinkle, twinkle, twinkle, twinkle_—” and went on so long that they
Sending: had to pinch it to make it stop.
Sending: 
Sending: “Well, I’d hardly finished the first verse,” said the Hatter, “when the
Sending: Queen jumped up and bawled out, ‘He’s murdering the time! Off with his
Sending: head!’”
Sending: 
Sending: “How dreadfully savage!” exclaimed Alice.
Sending: 
Sending: “And ever since that,” the Hatter went on in a mournful tone, “he won’t
Sending: do a thing I ask! It’s always six o’clock now.”
Sending: 
Sending: A bright idea came into Alice’s head. “Is that the reason so many
Sending: tea-things are put out here?” she asked.
Sending: 
Sending: “Yes, that’s it,” said the Hatter with a sigh: “it’s always tea-time,
Sending: and we’ve no time to wash the things between whiles.”
Sending: 
Sending: “Then you keep moving round, I suppose?” said Alice.
Sending: 
Sending: “Exactly so,” said the Hatter: “as the things get used up.”
Sending: 
Sending: “But what happens when you come to the beginning again?” Alice ventured
Sending: to ask.
Sending: 
Sending: “Suppose we change the subject,” the March Hare interrupted, yawning.
Sending: “I’m getting tired of this. I vote the young lady tells us a story.”
Sending: 
Sending: “I’m afraid I don’t know one,” said Alice, rather alarmed at the
Sending: proposal.
Sending: 
Sending: “Then the Dormouse shall!” they both cried. “Wake up, Dormouse!” And
Sending: they pinched it on both sides at once.
Sending: 
Sending: The Dormouse slowly opened his eyes. “I wasn’t asleep,” he said in a
Sending: hoarse, feeble voice: “I heard every word you fellows were saying.”
Sending: 
Sending: “Tell us a story!” said the March Hare.
Sending: 
Sending: “Yes, please do!” pleaded Alice.
Sending: 
Sending: “And be quick about it,” added the Hatter, “or you’ll be asleep again
Sending: before it’s done.”
Sending: 
Sending: “Once upon a time there were three little sisters,” the Dormouse began
Sending: in a great hurry; “and their names were Elsie, Lacie, and Tillie; and
Sending: they lived at the bottom of a well—”
Sending: 
Sending: “What did they live on?” said Alice, who always took a great interest
Sending: in questions of eating and drinking.
Sending: 
Sending: “They lived on treacle,” said the Dormouse, after thinking a minute or
Sending: two.
Sending: 
Sending: “They couldn’t have done that, you know,” Alice gently remarked;
Sending: “they’d have been ill.”
Sending: 
Sending: “So they were,” said the Dormouse; “_very_ ill.”
Sending: 
Sending: Alice tried to fancy to herself what such an extraordinary way of
Sending: living would be like, but it puzzled her too much, so she went on: “But
Sending: why did they live at the bottom of a well?”
Sending: 
Sending: “Take some more tea,” the March Hare said to Alice, very earnestly.
Sending: 
Sending: “I’ve had nothing yet,” Alice replied in an offended tone, “so I can’t
Sending: take more.”
Sending: 
Sending: “You mean you can’t take _less_,” said the Hatter: “it’s very easy to
Sending: take _more_ than nothing.”
Sending: 
Sending: “Nobody asked _your_ opinion,” said Alice.
Sending: 
Sending: “Who’s making personal remarks now?” the Hatter asked triumphantly.
Sending: 
Sending: Alice did not quite know what to say to this: so she helped herself to
Sending: some tea and bread-and-butter, and then turned to the Dormouse, and
Sending: repeated her question. “Why did they live at the bottom of a well?”
Sending: 
Sending: The Dormouse again took a minute or two to think about it, and then
Sending: said, “It was a treacle-well.”
Sending: 
Sending: “There’s no such thing!” Alice was beginning very angrily, but the
Sending: Hatter and the March Hare went “Sh! sh!” and the Dormouse sulkily
Sending: remarked, “If you can’t be civil, you’d better finish the story for
Sending: yourself.”
Sending: 
Sending: “No, please go on!” Alice said very humbly; “I won’t interrupt again. I
Sending: dare say there may be _one_.”
Sending: 
Sending: “One, indeed!” said the Dormouse indignantly. However, he consented to
Sending: go on. “And so these three little sisters—they were learning to draw,
Sending: you know—”
Sending: 
Sending: “What did they draw?” said Alice, quite forgetting her promise.
Sending: 
Sending: “Treacle,” said the Dormouse, without considering at all this time.
Sending: 
Sending: “I want a clean cup,” interrupted the Hatter: “let’s all move one place
Sending: on.”
Sending: 
Sending: He moved on as he spoke, and the Dormouse followed him: the March Hare
