Sending: place of the March Hare. The Hatter was the only one who got any
Sending: advantage from the change: and Alice was a good deal worse off than
Sending: before, as the March Hare had just upset the milk-jug into his plate.
Sending: 
Sending: Alice did not wish to offend the Dormouse again, so she began very
Sending: cautiously: “But I don’t understand. Where did they draw the treacle
Sending: from?”
Sending: 
Sending: “You can draw water out of a water-well,” said the Hatter; “so I should
Sending: think you could draw treacle out of a treacle-well—eh, stupid?”
Sending: 
Sending: “But they were _in_ the well,” Alice said to the Dormouse, not choosing
Sending: to notice this last remark.
Sending: 
Sending: “Of course they were,” said the Dormouse; “—well in.”
Sending: 
Sending: This answer so confused poor Alice, that she let the Dormouse go on for
Sending: some time without interrupting it.
Sending: 
Sending: “They were learning to draw,” the Dormouse went on, yawning and rubbing
Sending: its eyes, for it was getting very sleepy; “and they drew all manner of
Sending: things—everything that begins with an M—”
Sending: 
Sending: “Why with an M?” said Alice.
Sending: 
Sending: “Why not?” said the March Hare.
Sending: 
Sending: Alice was silent.
Sending: 
Sending: The Dormouse had closed its eyes by this time, and was going off into a
Sending: doze; but, on being pinched by the Hatter, it woke up again with a
