Sending: CHAPTER VIII.
Sending: The Queen’s Croquet-Ground
Sending: 
Sending: 
Sending: A large rose-tree stood near the entrance of the garden: the roses
Sending: growing on it were white, but there were three gardeners at it, busily
Sending: painting them red. Alice thought this a very curious thing, and she
Sending: went nearer to watch them, and just as she came up to them she heard
Sending: one of them say, “Look out now, Five! Don’t go splashing paint over me
Sending: like that!”
Sending: 
Sending: “I couldn’t help it,” said Five, in a sulky tone; “Seven jogged my
Sending: elbow.”
Sending: 
Sending: On which Seven looked up and said, “That’s right, Five! Always lay the
Sending: blame on others!”
Sending: 
Sending: “_You’d_ better not talk!” said Five. “I heard the Queen say only
Sending: yesterday you deserved to be beheaded!”
Sending: 
Sending: “What for?” said the one who had spoken first.
Sending: 
Sending: “That’s none of _your_ business, Two!” said Seven.
Sending: 
Sending: “Yes, it _is_ his business!” said Five, “and I’ll tell him—it was for
Sending: bringing the cook tulip-roots instead of onions.”
Sending: 
Sending: Seven flung down his brush, and had just begun “Well, of all the unjust
Sending: things—” when his eye chanced to fall upon Alice, as she stood watching
Sending: them, and he checked himself suddenly: the others looked round also,
Sending: and all of them bowed low.
Sending: 
Sending: “Would you tell me,” said Alice, a little timidly, “why you are
Sending: painting those roses?”
Sending: 
Sending: Five and Seven said nothing, but looked at Two. Two began in a low
Sending: voice, “Why the fact is, you see, Miss, this here ought to have been a
Sending: _red_ rose-tree, and we put a white one in by mistake; and if the Queen
Sending: was to find it out, we should all have our heads cut off, you know. So
Sending: you see, Miss, we’re doing our best, afore she comes, to—” At this
Sending: moment Five, who had been anxiously looking across the garden, called
Sending: out “The Queen! The Queen!” and the three gardeners instantly threw
Sending: themselves flat upon their faces. There was a sound of many footsteps,
Sending: and Alice looked round, eager to see the Queen.
Sending: 
Sending: First came ten soldiers carrying clubs; these were all shaped like the
Sending: three gardeners, oblong and flat, with their hands and feet at the
Sending: corners: next the ten courtiers; these were ornamented all over with
Sending: diamonds, and walked two and two, as the soldiers did. After these came
Sending: the royal children; there were ten of them, and the little dears came
Sending: jumping merrily along hand in hand, in couples: they were all
Sending: ornamented with hearts. Next came the guests, mostly Kings and Queens,
Sending: and among them Alice recognised the White Rabbit: it was talking in a
Sending: hurried nervous manner, smiling at everything that was said, and went
Sending: by without noticing her. Then followed the Knave of Hearts, carrying
Sending: the King’s crown on a crimson velvet cushion; and, last of all this
Sending: grand procession, came THE KING AND QUEEN OF HEARTS.
Sending: 
Sending: Alice was rather doubtful whether she ought not to lie down on her face
Sending: like the three gardeners, but she could not remember ever having heard
Sending: of such a rule at processions; “and besides, what would be the use of a
Sending: procession,” thought she, “if people had all to lie down upon their
Sending: faces, so that they couldn’t see it?” So she stood still where she was,
Sending: and waited.
Sending: 
Sending: When the procession came opposite to Alice, they all stopped and looked
Sending: at her, and the Queen said severely “Who is this?” She said it to the
Sending: Knave of Hearts, who only bowed and smiled in reply.
Sending: 
Sending: “Idiot!” said the Queen, tossing her head impatiently; and, turning to
Sending: Alice, she went on, “What’s your name, child?”
Sending: 
Sending: “My name is Alice, so please your Majesty,” said Alice very politely;
Sending: but she added, to herself, “Why, they’re only a pack of cards, after
Sending: all. I needn’t be afraid of them!”
Sending: 
Sending: “And who are _these?_” said the Queen, pointing to the three gardeners
Sending: who were lying round the rose-tree; for, you see, as they were lying on
