Sending: CHAPTER X.
Sending: The Lobster Quadrille
Sending: 
Sending: 
Sending: The Mock Turtle sighed deeply, and drew the back of one flapper across
Sending: his eyes. He looked at Alice, and tried to speak, but for a minute or
Sending: two sobs choked his voice. “Same as if he had a bone in his throat,”
Sending: said the Gryphon: and it set to work shaking him and punching him in
Sending: the back. At last the Mock Turtle recovered his voice, and, with tears
Sending: running down his cheeks, he went on again:—
Sending: 
Sending: “You may not have lived much under the sea—” (“I haven’t,” said
Sending: Alice)—“and perhaps you were never even introduced to a lobster—”
Sending: (Alice began to say “I once tasted—” but checked herself hastily, and
Sending: said “No, never”) “—so you can have no idea what a delightful thing a
Sending: Lobster Quadrille is!”
Sending: 
Sending: “No, indeed,” said Alice. “What sort of a dance is it?”
Sending: 
Sending: “Why,” said the Gryphon, “you first form into a line along the
Sending: sea-shore—”
Sending: 
Sending: “Two lines!” cried the Mock Turtle. “Seals, turtles, salmon, and so on;
Sending: then, when you’ve cleared all the jelly-fish out of the way—”
Sending: 
Sending: “_That_ generally takes some time,” interrupted the Gryphon.
Sending: 
Sending: “—you advance twice—”
Sending: 
Sending: “Each with a lobster as a partner!” cried the Gryphon.
Sending: 
Sending: “Of course,” the Mock Turtle said: “advance twice, set to partners—”
Sending: 
Sending: “—change lobsters, and retire in same order,” continued the Gryphon.
Sending: 
Sending: “Then, you know,” the Mock Turtle went on, “you throw the—”
Sending: 
Sending: “The lobsters!” shouted the Gryphon, with a bound into the air.
Sending: 
Sending: “—as far out to sea as you can—”
Sending: 
Sending: “Swim after them!” screamed the Gryphon.
Sending: 
Sending: “Turn a somersault in the sea!” cried the Mock Turtle, capering wildly
Sending: about.
Sending: 
Sending: “Change lobsters again!” yelled the Gryphon at the top of its voice.
Sending: 
Sending: “Back to land again, and that’s all the first figure,” said the Mock
Sending: Turtle, suddenly dropping his voice; and the two creatures, who had
Sending: been jumping about like mad things all this time, sat down again very
Sending: sadly and quietly, and looked at Alice.
Sending: 
Sending: “It must be a very pretty dance,” said Alice timidly.
Sending: 
Sending: “Would you like to see a little of it?” said the Mock Turtle.
Sending: 
Sending: “Very much indeed,” said Alice.
Sending: 
Sending: “Come, let’s try the first figure!” said the Mock Turtle to the
Sending: Gryphon. “We can do without lobsters, you know. Which shall sing?”
Sending: 
Sending: “Oh, _you_ sing,” said the Gryphon. “I’ve forgotten the words.”
Sending: 
Sending: So they began solemnly dancing round and round Alice, every now and
Sending: then treading on her toes when they passed too close, and waving their
Sending: forepaws to mark the time, while the Mock Turtle sang this, very slowly
Sending: and sadly:—
Sending: 
Sending: “Will you walk a little faster?” said a whiting to a snail.
Sending: “There’s a porpoise close behind us, and he’s treading on my tail.
Sending: See how eagerly the lobsters and the turtles all advance!
Sending: They are waiting on the shingle—will you come and join the dance?
Sending: Will you, won’t you, will you, won’t you, will you join the dance?
Sending: Will you, won’t you, will you, won’t you, won’t you join the dance?
Sending: 
Sending: “You can really have no notion how delightful it will be
Sending: When they take us up and throw us, with the lobsters, out to sea!”
Sending: But the snail replied “Too far, too far!” and gave a look askance—
Sending: Said he thanked the whiting kindly, but he would not join the dance.
Sending: Would not, could not, would not, could not, would not join the dance.
Sending: Would not, could not, would not, could not, could not join the dance.
Sending: 
Sending: “What matters it how far we go?” his scaly friend replied.
Sending: “There is another shore, you know, upon the other side.
Sending: The further off from England the nearer is to France—
Sending: Then turn not pale, beloved snail, but come and join the dance.
Sending: Will you, won’t you, will you, won’t you, will you join the dance?
Sending: Will you, won’t you, will you, won’t you, won’t you join the dance?”
Sending: 
Sending: 
Sending: “Thank you, it’s a very interesting dance to watch,” said Alice,
Sending: feeling very glad that it was over at last: “and I do so like that
Sending: curious song about the whiting!”
Sending: 
Sending: “Oh, as to the whiting,” said the Mock Turtle, “they—you’ve seen them,
Sending: of course?”
Sending: 
Sending: “Yes,” said Alice, “I’ve often seen them at dinn—” she checked herself
Sending: hastily.
Sending: 
Sending: “I don’t know where Dinn may be,” said the Mock Turtle, “but if you’ve
Sending: seen them so often, of course you know what they’re like.”
Sending: 
Sending: “I believe so,” Alice replied thoughtfully. “They have their tails in
Sending: their mouths—and they’re all over crumbs.”
Sending: 
Sending: “You’re wrong about the crumbs,” said the Mock Turtle: “crumbs would
Sending: all wash off in the sea. But they _have_ their tails in their mouths;
Sending: and the reason is—” here the Mock Turtle yawned and shut his
Sending: eyes.—“Tell her about the reason and all that,” he said to the Gryphon.
Sending: 
Sending: “The reason is,” said the Gryphon, “that they _would_ go with the
Sending: lobsters to the dance. So they got thrown out to sea. So they had to
Sending: fall a long way. So they got their tails fast in their mouths. So they
Sending: couldn’t get them out again. That’s all.”
Sending: 
Sending: “Thank you,” said Alice, “it’s very interesting. I never knew so much
Sending: about a whiting before.”
Sending: 
Sending: “I can tell you more than that, if you like,” said the Gryphon. “Do you
Sending: know why it’s called a whiting?”
Sending: 
Sending: “I never thought about it,” said Alice. “Why?”
Sending: 
Sending: “_It does the boots and shoes_,” the Gryphon replied very solemnly.
Sending: 
Sending: Alice was thoroughly puzzled. “Does the boots and shoes!” she repeated
Sending: in a wondering tone.
Sending: 
Sending: “Why, what are _your_ shoes done with?” said the Gryphon. “I mean, what
