Sending: CHAPTER XI.
Sending: Who Stole the Tarts?
Sending: 
Sending: 
Sending: The King and Queen of Hearts were seated on their throne when they
Sending: arrived, with a great crowd assembled about them—all sorts of little
Sending: birds and beasts, as well as the whole pack of cards: the Knave was
Sending: standing before them, in chains, with a soldier on each side to guard
Sending: him; and near the King was the White Rabbit, with a trumpet in one
Sending: hand, and a scroll of parchment in the other. In the very middle of the
Sending: court was a table, with a large dish of tarts upon it: they looked so
Sending: good, that it made Alice quite hungry to look at them—“I wish they’d
Sending: get the trial done,” she thought, “and hand round the refreshments!”
Sending: But there seemed to be no chance of this, so she began looking at
Sending: everything about her, to pass away the time.
Sending: 
Sending: Alice had never been in a court of justice before, but she had read
Sending: about them in books, and she was quite pleased to find that she knew
Sending: the name of nearly everything there. “That’s the judge,” she said to
Sending: herself, “because of his great wig.”
Sending: 
Sending: The judge, by the way, was the King; and as he wore his crown over the
Sending: wig, (look at the frontispiece if you want to see how he did it,) he
Sending: did not look at all comfortable, and it was certainly not becoming.
Sending: 
Sending: “And that’s the jury-box,” thought Alice, “and those twelve creatures,”
Sending: (she was obliged to say “creatures,” you see, because some of them were
Sending: animals, and some were birds,) “I suppose they are the jurors.” She
Sending: said this last word two or three times over to herself, being rather
Sending: proud of it: for she thought, and rightly too, that very few little
Sending: girls of her age knew the meaning of it at all. However, “jury-men”
Sending: would have done just as well.
Sending: 
Sending: The twelve jurors were all writing very busily on slates. “What are
Sending: they doing?” Alice whispered to the Gryphon. “They can’t have anything
Sending: to put down yet, before the trial’s begun.”
Sending: 
Sending: “They’re putting down their names,” the Gryphon whispered in reply,
Sending: “for fear they should forget them before the end of the trial.”
Sending: 
Sending: “Stupid things!” Alice began in a loud, indignant voice, but she
Sending: stopped hastily, for the White Rabbit cried out, “Silence in the
Sending: court!” and the King put on his spectacles and looked anxiously round,
Sending: to make out who was talking.
Sending: 
Sending: Alice could see, as well as if she were looking over their shoulders,
Sending: that all the jurors were writing down “stupid things!” on their slates,
Sending: and she could even make out that one of them didn’t know how to spell
Sending: “stupid,” and that he had to ask his neighbour to tell him. “A nice
Sending: muddle their slates’ll be in before the trial’s over!” thought Alice.
Sending: 
Sending: One of the jurors had a pencil that squeaked. This of course, Alice
Sending: could _not_ stand, and she went round the court and got behind him, and
Sending: very soon found an opportunity of taking it away. She did it so quickly
Sending: that the poor little juror (it was Bill, the Lizard) could not make out
Sending: at all what had become of it; so, after hunting all about for it, he
Sending: was obliged to write with one finger for the rest of the day; and this
Sending: was of very little use, as it left no mark on the slate.
Sending: 
Sending: “Herald, read the accusation!” said the King.
Sending: 
Sending: On this the White Rabbit blew three blasts on the trumpet, and then
Sending: unrolled the parchment scroll, and read as follows:—
Sending: 
Sending: “The Queen of Hearts, she made some tarts,
Sending:     All on a summer day:
Sending: The Knave of Hearts, he stole those tarts,
Sending:     And took them quite away!”
Sending: 
Sending: 
Sending: “Consider your verdict,” the King said to the jury.
Sending: 
Sending: “Not yet, not yet!” the Rabbit hastily interrupted. “There’s a great
Sending: deal to come before that!”
Sending: 
Sending: “Call the first witness,” said the King; and the White Rabbit blew
Sending: three blasts on the trumpet, and called out, “First witness!”
Sending: 
Sending: The first witness was the Hatter. He came in with a teacup in one hand
Sending: and a piece of bread-and-butter in the other. “I beg pardon, your
Sending: Majesty,” he began, “for bringing these in: but I hadn’t quite finished
Sending: my tea when I was sent for.”
Sending: 
Sending: “You ought to have finished,” said the King. “When did you begin?”
Sending: 
Sending: The Hatter looked at the March Hare, who had followed him into the
Sending: court, arm-in-arm with the Dormouse. “Fourteenth of March, I _think_ it
Sending: was,” he said.
Sending: 
Sending: “Fifteenth,” said the March Hare.
Sending: 
Sending: “Sixteenth,” added the Dormouse.
Sending: 
Sending: “Write that down,” the King said to the jury, and the jury eagerly
Sending: wrote down all three dates on their slates, and then added them up, and
Sending: reduced the answer to shillings and pence.
Sending: 
Sending: “Take off your hat,” the King said to the Hatter.
Sending: 
Sending: “It isn’t mine,” said the Hatter.
Sending: 
Sending: “_Stolen!_” the King exclaimed, turning to the jury, who instantly made
Sending: a memorandum of the fact.
Sending: 
Sending: “I keep them to sell,” the Hatter added as an explanation; “I’ve none
Sending: of my own. I’m a hatter.”
Sending: 
Sending: Here the Queen put on her spectacles, and began staring at the Hatter,
Sending: who turned pale and fidgeted.
Sending: 
Sending: “Give your evidence,” said the King; “and don’t be nervous, or I’ll
Sending: have you executed on the spot.”
Sending: 
Sending: This did not seem to encourage the witness at all: he kept shifting
Sending: from one foot to the other, looking uneasily at the Queen, and in his
Sending: confusion he bit a large piece out of his teacup instead of the
Sending: bread-and-butter.
Sending: 
Sending: Just at this moment Alice felt a very curious sensation, which puzzled
Sending: her a good deal until she made out what it was: she was beginning to
Sending: grow larger again, and she thought at first she would get up and leave
Sending: the court; but on second thoughts she decided to remain where she was
Sending: as long as there was room for her.
