Sending: 
Sending: “—and just take his head off outside,” the Queen added to one of the
Sending: officers: but the Hatter was out of sight before the officer could get
Sending: to the door.
Sending: 
Sending: “Call the next witness!” said the King.
Sending: 
Sending: The next witness was the Duchess’s cook. She carried the pepper-box in
Sending: her hand, and Alice guessed who it was, even before she got into the
Sending: court, by the way the people near the door began sneezing all at once.
Sending: 
Sending: “Give your evidence,” said the King.
Sending: 
Sending: “Shan’t,” said the cook.
Sending: 
Sending: The King looked anxiously at the White Rabbit, who said in a low voice,
Sending: “Your Majesty must cross-examine _this_ witness.”
Sending: 
Sending: “Well, if I must, I must,” the King said, with a melancholy air, and,
Sending: after folding his arms and frowning at the cook till his eyes were
Sending: nearly out of sight, he said in a deep voice, “What are tarts made of?”
Sending: 
Sending: “Pepper, mostly,” said the cook.
Sending: 
Sending: “Treacle,” said a sleepy voice behind her.
Sending: 
Sending: “Collar that Dormouse,” the Queen shrieked out. “Behead that Dormouse!
Sending: Turn that Dormouse out of court! Suppress him! Pinch him! Off with his
Sending: whiskers!”
Sending: 
Sending: For some minutes the whole court was in confusion, getting the Dormouse
Sending: turned out, and, by the time they had settled down again, the cook had
Sending: disappeared.
Sending: 
Sending: “Never mind!” said the King, with an air of great relief. “Call the
Sending: next witness.” And he added in an undertone to the Queen, “Really, my
Sending: dear, _you_ must cross-examine the next witness. It quite makes my
Sending: forehead ache!”
Sending: 
Sending: Alice watched the White Rabbit as he fumbled over the list, feeling
Sending: very curious to see what the next witness would be like, “—for they
Sending: haven’t got much evidence _yet_,” she said to herself. Imagine her
Sending: surprise, when the White Rabbit read out, at the top of his shrill
Sending: little voice, the name “Alice!”
Sending: 
Sending: 
Sending: 
Sending: 
Sending: CHAPTER XII.
Sending: Alice’s Evidence
Sending: 
Sending: 
Sending: “Here!” cried Alice, quite forgetting in the flurry of the moment how
Sending: large she had grown in the last few minutes, and she jumped up in such
Sending: a hurry that she tipped over the jury-box with the edge of her skirt,
Sending: upsetting all the jurymen on to the heads of the crowd below, and there
Sending: they lay sprawling about, reminding her very much of a globe of
Sending: goldfish she had accidentally upset the week before.
Sending: 
Sending: “Oh, I _beg_ your pardon!” she exclaimed in a tone of great dismay, and
Sending: began picking them up again as quickly as she could, for the accident
Sending: of the goldfish kept running in her head, and she had a vague sort of
Sending: idea that they must be collected at once and put back into the
Sending: jury-box, or they would die.
Sending: 
Sending: “The trial cannot proceed,” said the King in a very grave voice, “until
Sending: all the jurymen are back in their proper places—_all_,” he repeated
Sending: with great emphasis, looking hard at Alice as he said so.
Sending: 
Sending: Alice looked at the jury-box, and saw that, in her haste, she had put
Sending: the Lizard in head downwards, and the poor little thing was waving its
Sending: tail about in a melancholy way, being quite unable to move. She soon
Sending: got it out again, and put it right; “not that it signifies much,” she
Sending: said to herself; “I should think it would be _quite_ as much use in the
Sending: trial one way up as the other.”
Sending: 
Sending: As soon as the jury had a little recovered from the shock of being
Sending: upset, and their slates and pencils had been found and handed back to
Sending: them, they set to work very diligently to write out a history of the
Sending: accident, all except the Lizard, who seemed too much overcome to do
Sending: anything but sit with its mouth open, gazing up into the roof of the
Sending: court.
Sending: 
Sending: “What do you know about this business?” the King said to Alice.
Sending: 
Sending: “Nothing,” said Alice.
Sending: 
Sending: “Nothing _whatever?_” persisted the King.
Sending: 
Sending: “Nothing whatever,” said Alice.
Sending: 
Sending: “That’s very important,” the King said, turning to the jury. They were
Sending: just beginning to write this down on their slates, when the White
Sending: Rabbit interrupted: “_Un_important, your Majesty means, of course,” he
Sending: said in a very respectful tone, but frowning and making faces at him as
Sending: he spoke.
Sending: 
Sending: “_Un_important, of course, I meant,” the King hastily said, and went on
Sending: to himself in an undertone,
Sending: 
Sending: “important—unimportant—unimportant—important—” as if he were trying
Sending: which word sounded best.
Sending: 
Sending: Some of the jury wrote it down “important,” and some “unimportant.”
Sending: Alice could see this, as she was near enough to look over their slates;
Sending: “but it doesn’t matter a bit,” she thought to herself.
Sending: 
Sending: At this moment the King, who had been for some time busily writing in
Sending: his note-book, cackled out “Silence!” and read out from his book, “Rule
Sending: Forty-two. _All persons more than a mile high to leave the court_.”
Sending: 
Sending: Everybody looked at Alice.
Sending: 
Sending: “_I’m_ not a mile high,” said Alice.
Sending: 
Sending: “You are,” said the King.
Sending: 
Sending: “Nearly two miles high,” added the Queen.
Sending: 
Sending: “Well, I shan’t go, at any rate,” said Alice: “besides, that’s not a
Sending: regular rule: you invented it just now.”
Sending: 
Sending: “It’s the oldest rule in the book,” said the King.
Sending: 
Sending: “Then it ought to be Number One,” said Alice.
Sending: 
Sending: The King turned pale, and shut his note-book hastily. “Consider your
Sending: verdict,” he said to the jury, in a low, trembling voice.
Sending: 
Sending: “There’s more evidence to come yet, please your Majesty,” said the
Sending: White Rabbit, jumping up in a great hurry; “this paper has just been
Sending: picked up.”
Sending: 
Sending: “What’s in it?” said the Queen.
Sending: 
Sending: “I haven’t opened it yet,” said the White Rabbit, “but it seems to be a
Sending: letter, written by the prisoner to—to somebody.”
Sending: 
Sending: “It must have been that,” said the King, “unless it was written to
Sending: nobody, which isn’t usual, you know.”
Sending: 
Sending: “Who is it directed to?” said one of the jurymen.
Sending: 
Sending: “It isn’t directed at all,” said the White Rabbit; “in fact, there’s
Sending: nothing written on the _outside_.” He unfolded the paper as he spoke,
Sending: and added “It isn’t a letter, after all: it’s a set of verses.”
Sending: 
Sending: “Are they in the prisoner’s handwriting?” asked another of the jurymen.
Sending: 
Sending: “No, they’re not,” said the White Rabbit, “and that’s the queerest
Sending: thing about it.” (The jury all looked puzzled.)
Sending: 
Sending: “He must have imitated somebody else’s hand,” said the King. (The jury
Sending: all brightened up again.)
Sending: 
Sending: “Please your Majesty,” said the Knave, “I didn’t write it, and they
Sending: can’t prove I did: there’s no name signed at the end.”
Sending: 
Sending: “If you didn’t sign it,” said the King, “that only makes the matter
Sending: worse. You _must_ have meant some mischief, or else you’d have signed
Sending: your name like an honest man.”
Sending: 
Sending: There was a general clapping of hands at this: it was the first really
Sending: clever thing the King had said that day.
Sending: 
Sending: “That _proves_ his guilt,” said the Queen.
Sending: 
Sending: “It proves nothing of the sort!” said Alice. “Why, you don’t even know
Sending: what they’re about!”
Sending: 
Sending: “Read them,” said the King.
Sending: 
Sending: The White Rabbit put on his spectacles. “Where shall I begin, please
Sending: your Majesty?” he asked.
Sending: 
Sending: “Begin at the beginning,” the King said gravely, “and go on till you
Sending: come to the end: then stop.”
Sending: 
Sending: These were the verses the White Rabbit read:—
Sending: 
Sending: “They told me you had been to her,
Sending:     And mentioned me to him:
Sending: She gave me a good character,
Sending:     But said I could not swim.
Sending: 
Sending: He sent them word I had not gone
Sending:     (We know it to be true):
Sending: If she should push the matter on,
Sending:     What would become of you?
Sending: 
Sending: I gave her one, they gave him two,
Sending:     You gave us three or more;
Sending: They all returned from him to you,
Sending:     Though they were mine before.
Sending: 
Sending: If I or she should chance to be
Sending:     Involved in this affair,
Sending: He trusts to you to set them free,
Sending:     Exactly as we were.
Sending: 
Sending: My notion was that you had been
Sending:     (Before she had this fit)
Sending: An obstacle that came between
Sending:     Him, and ourselves, and it.
Sending: 
Sending: Don’t let him know she liked them best,
Sending:     For this must ever be
Sending: A secret, kept from all the rest,
Sending:     Between yourself and me.”
Sending: 
Sending: 
Sending: “That’s the most important piece of evidence we’ve heard yet,” said the
Sending: King, rubbing his hands; “so now let the jury—”
Sending: 
Sending: “If any one of them can explain it,” said Alice, (she had grown so
Sending: large in the last few minutes that she wasn’t a bit afraid of
Sending: interrupting him,) “I’ll give him sixpence. _I_ don’t believe there’s
Sending: an atom of meaning in it.”
Sending: 
Sending: The jury all wrote down on their slates, “_She_ doesn’t believe there’s
Sending: an atom of meaning in it,” but none of them attempted to explain the
Sending: paper.
Sending: 
Sending: “If there’s no meaning in it,” said the King, “that saves a world of
Sending: trouble, you know, as we needn’t try to find any. And yet I don’t
Sending: know,” he went on, spreading out the verses on his knee, and looking at
Sending: them with one eye; “I seem to see some meaning in them, after all.
Sending: “—_said I could not swim_—” you can’t swim, can you?” he added, turning
Sending: to the Knave.
Sending: 
Sending: The Knave shook his head sadly. “Do I look like it?” he said. (Which he
Sending: certainly did _not_, being made entirely of cardboard.)
Sending: 
Sending: “All right, so far,” said the King, and he went on muttering over the
Sending: verses to himself: “‘_We know it to be true_—’ that’s the jury, of
Sending: course—‘_I gave her one, they gave him two_—’ why, that must be what he
Sending: did with the tarts, you know—”
Sending: 
Sending: “But, it goes on ‘_they all returned from him to you_,’” said Alice.
Sending: 
Sending: “Why, there they are!” said the King triumphantly, pointing to the
Sending: tarts on the table. “Nothing can be clearer than _that_. Then
Sending: again—‘_before she had this fit_—’ you never had fits, my dear, I
Sending: think?” he said to the Queen.
Sending: 
Sending: “Never!” said the Queen furiously, throwing an inkstand at the Lizard
Sending: as she spoke. (The unfortunate little Bill had left off writing on his
Sending: slate with one finger, as he found it made no mark; but he now hastily
Sending: began again, using the ink, that was trickling down his face, as long
Sending: as it lasted.)
Sending: 
Sending: “Then the words don’t _fit_ you,” said the King, looking round the
Sending: court with a smile. There was a dead silence.
Sending: 
Sending: “It’s a pun!” the King added in an offended tone, and everybody
Sending: laughed, “Let the jury consider their verdict,” the King said, for
Sending: about the twentieth time that day.
Sending: 
Sending: “No, no!” said the Queen. “Sentence first—verdict afterwards.”
Sending: 
Sending: “Stuff and nonsense!” said Alice loudly. “The idea of having the
Sending: sentence first!”
Sending: 
Sending: “Hold your tongue!” said the Queen, turning purple.
Sending: 
Sending: “I won’t!” said Alice.
Sending: 
Sending: “Off with her head!” the Queen shouted at the top of her voice. Nobody
Sending: moved.
Sending: 
Sending: “Who cares for you?” said Alice, (she had grown to her full size by
Sending: this time.) “You’re nothing but a pack of cards!”
Sending: 
Sending: At this the whole pack rose up into the air, and came flying down upon
Sending: her: she gave a little scream, half of fright and half of anger, and
Sending: tried to beat them off, and found herself lying on the bank, with her
Sending: head in the lap of her sister, who was gently brushing away some dead
Sending: leaves that had fluttered down from the trees upon her face.
Sending: 
Sending: “Wake up, Alice dear!” said her sister; “Why, what a long sleep you’ve
Sending: had!”
Sending: 
Sending: “Oh, I’ve had such a curious dream!” said Alice, and she told her
Sending: sister, as well as she could remember them, all these strange
Sending: Adventures of hers that you have just been reading about; and when she
Sending: had finished, her sister kissed her, and said, “It _was_ a curious
Sending: dream, dear, certainly: but now run in to your tea; it’s getting late.”
Sending: So Alice got up and ran off, thinking while she ran, as well she might,
Sending: what a wonderful dream it had been.
Sending: 
Sending: 
Sending: But her sister sat still just as she left her, leaning her head on her
Sending: hand, watching the setting sun, and thinking of little Alice and all
Sending: her wonderful Adventures, till she too began dreaming after a fashion,
Sending: and this was her dream:—
Sending: 
Sending: First, she dreamed of little Alice herself, and once again the tiny
Sending: hands were clasped upon her knee, and the bright eager eyes were
Sending: looking up into hers—she could hear the very tones of her voice, and
Sending: see that queer little toss of her head to keep back the wandering hair
Sending: that _would_ always get into her eyes—and still as she listened, or
Sending: seemed to listen, the whole place around her became alive with the
Sending: strange creatures of her little sister’s dream.
Sending: 
Sending: The long grass rustled at her feet as the White Rabbit hurried by—the
Sending: frightened Mouse splashed his way through the neighbouring pool—she
Sending: could hear the rattle of the teacups as the March Hare and his friends
Sending: shared their never-ending meal, and the shrill voice of the Queen
Sending: ordering off her unfortunate guests to execution—once more the pig-baby
Sending: was sneezing on the Duchess’s knee, while plates and dishes crashed
Sending: around it—once more the shriek of the Gryphon, the squeaking of the
Sending: Lizard’s slate-pencil, and the choking of the suppressed guinea-pigs,
Sending: filled the air, mixed up with the distant sobs of the miserable Mock
Sending: Turtle.
Sending: 
Sending: So she sat on, with closed eyes, and half believed herself in
Sending: Wonderland, though she knew she had but to open them again, and all
Sending: would change to dull reality—the grass would be only rustling in the
Sending: wind, and the pool rippling to the waving of the reeds—the rattling
Sending: teacups would change to tinkling sheep-bells, and the Queen’s shrill
Sending: cries to the voice of the shepherd boy—and the sneeze of the baby, the
Sending: shriek of the Gryphon, and all the other queer noises, would change
Sending: (she knew) to the confused clamour of the busy farm-yard—while the
Sending: lowing of the cattle in the distance would take the place of the Mock
Sending: Turtle’s heavy sobs.
Sending: 
Sending: Lastly, she pictured to herself how this same little sister of hers
Sending: would, in the after-time, be herself a grown woman; and how she would
Sending: keep, through all her riper years, the simple and loving heart of her
Sending: childhood: and how she would gather about her other little children,
Sending: and make _their_ eyes bright and eager with many a strange tale,
Sending: perhaps even with the dream of Wonderland of long ago: and how she
Sending: would feel with all their simple sorrows, and find a pleasure in all
Sending: their simple joys, remembering her own child-life, and the happy summer
Sending: days.
Sending: 
Sending: THE END
